# SSD private mode (ADR-0017, v2.8.0+). NOTHING SSD produces is tracked by git.
#
# CANONICAL SINGLE SOURCE. `ssd-init/SKILL.md` Step 5 and `methodology/migrate.sh`
# (apply_private_mode) append this file's contents verbatim. Edit only here.
#
# Deliberately contains NO `!` negation — not even `!.ssd/gate.yml` (ADR-0015's committed gate
# inputs cannot exist under this mode; see ADR-0017 § "What we give up"). Selective mode needs a
# precise allow-list because it commits a subset; private mode commits nothing, so there is no
# allow-list to get wrong.
#
# The sentinel line below is load-bearing: `ssd-init` Step 5.5 runs BEFORE Step 6 writes
# project.yml, so at that point in the init flow the mode can only be read off .gitignore. No
# functional line here is distinctive enough to serve (a bare `.ssd/` is indistinguishable from
# blanket mode), hence a comment sentinel. Detection order must be private -> selective -> blanket.
# ssd:gitignore-mode=private
# A BARE `.ssd` LINE, in addition to `.ssd/` below (ADR-0018). Not redundant: a gitignore pattern with
# a TRAILING SLASH matches DIRECTORIES ONLY, and to git a symlink is a FILE — so `.ssd/` cannot match a
# `.ssd` symlink, which is what the artifact store makes it. Verified: without this line
# `git check-ignore .ssd` fails and `git add -A` commits the symlink, publishing the absolute store path
# (the user's home directory) into the repository they are keeping private.
#
# THIS LINE BELONGS ONLY HERE, NEVER IN selective.gitignore. A bare `.ssd` excludes the DIRECTORY, and
# gitignore cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded — so in selective mode it makes
# every `!.ssd/features/**/…` negation inert and the project commits NOTHING under .ssd/. Verified by
# regression, and pinned by the `selective-artifacts-still-committable` fixture. Private mode excludes
# the whole tree anyway, so here the directory-exclusion side effect changes nothing.
.ssd
.ssd/
docs/decisions/
docs/runbooks/
docs/architecture/
